# CoreMark, vendored unmodified from eembc/coremark at commit
# 1f483d5b8316753a742cbf5590caf5bd0a4e4777 (2025-05-01), Apache-2.0. LICENSE.md
# carries both that grant and the separate CoreMark(R) trademark agreement,
# whose Article 1.2 is why these files stay untouched: the mark may be used
# only "in connection with the generation of data" from an unmodified copy of
# the software. The porting layer that adapts it to this core -- core_portme.h,
# coremark_port.c, coremark.lds -- lives one directory up and is this repo's
# own code, not EEMBC's.
#
# Checked against `shasum -a 256` on every `make coremark` run, because
# upstream's own coremark.md5 cannot be trusted for this: its coremark.h entry
# claims 8ca974c013b380dc7f0d6d1afb76eb2d, and a byte-identical fetch of
# coremark.h at this same commit hashes to b0ec69b6c8e75853d06accb3b1bcf534
# instead -- upstream never regenerated coremark.md5 after a later edit to
# coremark.h. The other five entries in coremark.md5 are correct; this is a
# one-file staleness bug in the tree it shipped from, not tampering, and
# `make revendor-coremark` reproduces it on demand rather than leaving it as
# an assertion. This manifest is what this repo vendored, verified against
# the pin by that target, not a re-statement of a file taken on faith.
